---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Rule of 72 Explained — Fast Mental Math for Doubling Money"
  description="Learn how the Rule of 72 works, its mathematical derivation from natural logarithms, when it is accurate, and how to use it for investing and inflation."
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
    { name: 'Learn', url: '/learn/' },
    { name: 'Rule of 72 Guide', url: '/guides/rule-of-72/' }
  ]}
>
  <div class="guide-article-page">
    <div class="container article-container">
      <nav class="breadcrumb-nav">
        <a href={`${base}/`}>Home</a> / <a href={`${base}/learn/`}>Learn</a> / <span>Rule of 72</span>
      </nav>

      <span class="badge badge-emerald">Mental Financial Models</span>
      <h1>The Rule of 72: How to Double Your Money</h1>
      <p class="article-lead">
        The Rule of 72 is one of the most famous heuristics in finance. It allows you to rapidly estimate the number of years required to double your investment at a fixed annual rate of return without needing a calculator.
      </p>

      <div class="article-body">
        <h2>The Core Formula</h2>
        <div class="formula-callout">
          <code>Years to Double ≈ 72 / Expected Annual Return (%)</code>
        </div>
        <p>
          For example:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>At <strong>6% annual return</strong> (Fixed Deposits): 72 / 6 = <strong>12 Years</strong> to double.</li>
          <li>At <strong>8% annual return</strong> (Balanced Funds): 72 / 8 = <strong>9 Years</strong> to double.</li>
          <li>At <strong>12% annual return</strong> (Equity Index Funds): 72 / 12 = <strong>6 Years</strong> to double.</li>
          <li>At <strong>18% annual return</strong> (High Growth Stocks): 72 / 18 = <strong>4 Years</strong> to double.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Mathematical Derivation</h2>
        <p>
          Where does the number 72 come from? It originates from continuous compounding:
        </p>
        <p>
          If an initial investment $P$ doubles to $2P$ at continuous rate $r$ in time $t$:
        </p>
        <div class="math-proof">
          2P = P × e^(rt)<br/>
          2 = e^(rt)<br/>
          ln(2) = rt<br/>
          0.6931 = rt<br/>
          t = 0.6931 / r
        </div>
        <p>
          For discrete annual compounding at rates between 6% and 10%, 72 is chosen over 69.3 because 72 has many convenient divisors (2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 9, 12, 18, 24, 36), making mental calculation instantaneous.
        </p>

        <h2>Applying the Rule of 72 in Reverse (Inflation Halving Time)</h2>
        <p>
          You can also apply the Rule of 72 to inflation to determine how fast your purchasing power is cut in half:
        </p>
        <div class="formula-callout">
          <code>Years to Halve Purchasing Power ≈ 72 / Inflation Rate (%)</code>
        </div>
        <p>
          At an average inflation rate of <strong>6%</strong>, the real value of your cash drops by 50% in approximately <strong>12 years</strong>.
        </p>

        <div class="cta-box">
          <h3>Test the Exact Math</h3>
          <p>Don't settle for estimates when you can get exact days and months with monthly contributions.</p>
          <a href={`${base}/calculators/time-to-money-calculator/`} class="btn btn-primary">
            Launch Time to Target Calculator →
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .guide-article-page {
    padding: 3rem 0 5rem;
  }

  .article-container {
    max-width: 780px;
  }

  .breadcrumb-nav {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .breadcrumb-nav a:hover {
    color: var(--accent-primary);
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0.75rem 0 1.25rem;
  }

  .article-lead {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  .article-body h2 {
    font-size: 1.45rem;
    margin: 2rem 0 1rem;
  }

  .article-body p {
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }

  .article-body ul {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem 1.5rem;
    font-size: 0.98rem;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  .article-body li {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .formula-callout {
    background: var(--bg-tertiary);
    border-left: 4px solid var(--accent-primary);
    padding: 1.25rem;
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    margin: 1.25rem 0;
  }

  .formula-callout code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: var(--accent-primary);
  }

  .math-proof {
    background: var(--bg-card);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    padding: 1.25rem;
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    margin: 1rem 0;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .cta-box {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    background: var(--bg-card);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-medium);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    padding: 2rem;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .cta-box h3 {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .cta-box p {
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }
</style>
